※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `&T` is a name for a place someone else holds, and `&mut T` one the place may
※ be written through.  `&x` makes one out of a place, and `x⌖` reads what is at
※ the place one names.
※
※ **Two `mut`s that say two things.**  `mut` before a type says the *name* may
※ be bound to something else, which is what it has always said here; `mut`
※ inside a reference type says the *place* may be written.  So
※ `let r: mut &mut i8` is a reference that may be pointed elsewhere at a place
※ that may be written, and each half of that is decided by its own word.  A
※ reference is part of a type because both the one who wrote it and the one who
※ reads it reach the place; a name is not shared, so what may be done to a name
※ is no part of anything.
※
※ **The mark stands after what it reads through**, so reaching further into
※ what it answers -- an element of it -- reads left to right without brackets.
※ That is what Pascal, Modula, Ada and Odin put a mark after a pointer for.
※
※ **Whether it may be written is part of the type, so the context says it.**
※ `&x` bound to a `&mut T` is a reference that may write, exactly as an integer
※ literal bound to a `u8` is a `u8`; `mut` is written where nothing says -- a name
※ whose type is read off its value -- and says it outright wherever a reader wants
※ it said.  The place must still allow it either way (4537).
※
※ **A name may hold a reference only to a place that lasts as long as it
※ does** (4570).  A name bound further out lasts longer than a place made
※ further in, so giving it a reference to one would leave it naming a variable
※ the program can no longer name.  Inward is always allowed, which is what the
※ arm below does.  The same is asked of everything that can reach a place: a
※ tuple holding a reference, and a lambda, which reaches what it brought in by
※ reference.
※
※ **A place the call did not make takes only a lasting reference** (4571).  How
※ long such a place lasts is the caller's business, so a reference of this call's
※ own put there would outlive the call that made it -- and neither end can see
※ it.
※
※ **A `&mut` is the only reference to its place while it lives**, and a `&` may
※ share with other `&`s.  So each variable here is lent once: while `r` is out,
※ `n` is reached through `r` and not by its own name, and a second `&mut n`
※ would be refused.  A reference nothing binds lives only to the end of the
※ statement that made it, which is why `bump(&mut p)` may stand twice.

let counter: mut u16 = 0u16

enum Colour:
    red ;
    green

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    let n: mut i8 = 1i8
    ※ a reference to a name, read and written through
    let r: &mut i8 = &mut n
    r⌖ ← 3i8
    let seen: i8 = r⌖ + 1i8
    ※ one that may be pointed elsewhere, which is what mut on the binding says
    let a: mut i8 = 3i8
    let m: mut i8 = 10i8
    let moving: mut &mut i8 = &mut a
    let first: i8 = moving⌖
    moving ← &mut m
    moving⌖ ← 20i8
    ※ a parameter, which is what a reference is mostly for: the callee writes
    ※ the caller's variable and the call says so.  The reference is nobody's
    ※ once the call is over, so the next statement may make another
    let p: mut i8 = 28i8
    bump(&mut p)
    ※ and once with no `mut` written, the parameter's type saying it
    bump(&p)
    ※ two that may not be written through, sharing the same place
    let only_at: i8 = 30i8
    let only: &i8 = &only_at
    let also: &i8 = &only_at
    ※ and one that may be written through, standing where one that may not is
    ※ wanted: it promises everything the other does and more
    let w: mut i8 = 12i8
    let writable: &mut i8 = &mut w
    let readable: &i8 = writable
    ※ an element of an array, which is a place like any other
    let v: mut u8⟦4⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8, 4u8⟧
    let e: &mut u8 = &mut v⟦2⟧
    e⌖ ← 9u8
    ※ a name given a reference to a place of the same scope, and one of an
    ※ outer scope, both of which last as long as the name does
    let far: mut i8 = 60i8
    let reaching: mut &mut i8 = &far
    if only⌖ = 30i8:
        ※ outward, which is allowed: what it names outlives the name
        reaching⌖ ← reaching⌖ + 1i8
        ※ and a name of this scope naming a place of this scope
        let near: mut i8 = 70i8
        let alongside: &mut i8 = &near
        alongside⌖ ← 71i8
    ※ a tuple holding one, and a lambda that brought one in: both reach what
    ※ a name of this scope stands for and last as long as it does
    let inside: mut i8 = 61i8
    let paired: 〈&mut i8, u8〉 = 〈&inside, 1u8〉
    let watched: mut i8 = 61i8
    let watching: fn() → i8 = λ [&watched] → i8 { watched }
    ※ a variable at the top level, which is an address already
    let g: &mut u16 = &mut counter
    g⌖ ← 7u16
    ※ a reference to a reference, which is a place holding a place
    ※ the declared type saying it, with no `mut` on the right
    let told: mut i8 = 12i8
    let quiet: &mut i8 = &told
    quiet⌖ ← 13i8
    let d: mut i8 = 1i8
    let deep: mut &mut i8 = &mut d
    let over: &mut &mut i8 = &mut deep
    over⌖⌖ ← 30i8
    ※ every other kind of value a place holds as one
    let c: mut char = 'a'
    let t: mut bool = false
    let f: mut f64 = 1.5
    let hue: mut Colour = Colour.red
    set(&mut c, &mut t, &mut f, &mut hue)
    if r⌖ ≠ 3i8:
        1u6
    elif seen ≠ 4i8:
        2u6
    elif first ≠ 3i8 ∨ moving⌖ ≠ 20i8:
        3u6
    elif only⌖ ≠ 30i8 ∨ also⌖ ≠ 30i8:
        4u6
    elif readable⌖ ≠ 12i8 ∨ reads(writable) ≠ 12i8:
        11u6
    elif e⌖ ≠ 9u8:
        5u6
    elif counter ≠ 7u16:
        6u6
    elif c ≠ 'z' ∨ ¬t ∨ f ≇ 2.5 ∨ hue ≠ Colour.green:
        7u6
    ※ handed on from one call to the next, still naming the same place
    elif twice(&mut counter) ≠ 9u16:
        8u6
    elif over⌖⌖ ≠ 30i8:
        9u6
    elif reaching⌖ ≠ 61i8:
        13u6
    ※ a tuple holding one, and a lambda that brought one in, both bound to
    ※ names of this scope and so both allowed
    elif paired⟦0⟧⌖ ≠ 61i8 ∨ watching() ≠ 61i8:
        14u6
    ※ written through a reference the context made writable, and read the other
    ※ way round, a literal on the left taking its type from what the reference names
    elif 13i8 ≠ quiet⌖:
        12u6
    ※ bumped twice, each call lending the place for its own statement only
    elif bump_read(&mut p) ≠ 31i8:
        10u6
    else:
        0u6

※ A function that only reads takes the reference that only reads, and a caller
※ holding one that may write hands it over unchanged.
fn reads(at: &i8) → i8:
    at⌖

※ The callee writes what the caller named, which is the whole point.
@[impure]
fn bump(at: &mut i8):
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + 1i8

@[impure]
fn bump_read(at: &mut i8) → i8:
    bump(at)
    at⌖

@[impure]
fn set(c: &mut char, t: &mut bool, f: &mut f64, hue: &mut Colour):
    c⌖ ← 'z'
    t⌖ ← true
    f⌖ ← f⌖ + 1.0
    hue⌖ ← Colour.green

※ A reference handed on names the same place however many calls it passes
※ through: it is the place and not a copy of what is in it.
@[impure]
fn twice(at: &mut u16) → u16:
    bump_by(at, 1u16)
    bump_by(at, 1u16)
    at⌖

@[impure]
fn bump_by(at: &mut u16, by: u16):
    at⌖ ← at⌖ + by
